  "releaseNotes": "- [Honor ConfigureAwait(false) in the task builders](https://github.com/demystifyfp/FsToolkit.ErrorHandling/pull/399) Credits @TheAngryByrd\n- fix: in `taskResult`, `taskValidation`, `taskOption`, `taskValueOption`, `valueTaskValueOption`, and their `background` variants, a bind (`let!`, `do!`, `use!`, `match!`, `return!`) of a plain awaitable now awaits the awaiter of that awaitable, as `task { }` does. Before, the builder awaited a new task that wrapped the awaitable. Fixes [#398](https://github.com/demystifyfp/FsToolkit.ErrorHandling/issues/398).\n- Behavior change: when such an awaitable has `ConfigureAwait(false)` and is not complete, the builder does not request a return to the captured `SynchronizationContext` after the bind. Before, it did. UI code that touches a control after such a bind, including in a `finally` block after `return!`, must remove `ConfigureAwait(false)` or return to the UI thread itself.\n- Behavior change: in `let!`, `do!`, `use!`, `match!`, and `return!`, the `GetAwaiter` member of a custom awaitable now runs in the computation expression, as in `task { }`. An `AsyncLocal` value that it sets is now visible to the code after the bind. In `and!`, `GetAwaiter` still runs in a separate task.\n- Behavior change: `and!` of a plain awaitable now gets its awaiter after F# evaluates all `and!` operands. Each operand still starts without waiting for an earlier operand to complete, so the operands run at the same time. With more than two operands, F# nests the merges, so the last two operands get their awaiters first: for three operands, the order is 2, 3, 1. Before, each operand got its awaiter, and registered its continuation if it was not complete, before F# evaluated the next operand. If an operand expression throws, the builder does not await the operands before it. Code that observes when `GetAwaiter`, `IsCompleted`, or `OnCompleted` runs can see the difference, for example a custom awaitable or a `SynchronizationContext` that counts its posts.\n- `and!` still continues on the captured context, as `and!` in `task { }` does.\n- BREAKING (source and binary): the `Source` member for a plain awaitable now returns `PlainAwaitable<_>`, not a task. In `taskResult` and `taskValidation`, it also has one type parameter less. Code that calls this `Source` directly, and compiled quotations of such a call, must change. Extension members that a consumer adds to the builder types and that take the task that this `Source` returned before (a custom `Bind`, `BindReturn`, `ReturnFrom`, or `MergeSources`) no longer apply to plain awaitables. F# then selects the library member, so such code can compile and give a different value.\n- The changed members are `inline`. Assemblies that were compiled against an earlier version keep the old behavior until they are compiled again.",
